window.docContents[206].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___38-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions___-Further-Properties/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___38-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions___-Further-Properties--___4___38-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions___-Further-Properties--Power-series":{"contents":"E1 is the |z|<1 Maclaurin series for arcsinh.  E2 and E3 are the large-\nargument logarithmic expansions, with Re z>0 for E2 and |z|>1 for E3;\nE4 is the local arccosh expansion on Re z>0, |z-1|≤2, with its summation\nstarting at n=1.  E5–E7 give three\ncharts for arctanh, with their exact disk, half-plane, and hyperbola domains.\nIn E7 the coefficient is the product ratio 2·4···(2n+2)/(3·5···(2n+3)),\nnot a geometric surrogate.\n\n\\operatorname{arcsinh}z=z-\\frac{z^3}{2\\cdot3}+\\frac{1\\cdot3}{2\\cdot4}\\frac{z^5}{5}-\\cdots,\\quad |z|<1.$$\n\nThe source links are E2, E3, E4, E5, E6, E7, and E8.  The declarations below preserve each domain and series chart.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.38 Inverse Hyperbolic Functions: Further Properties\u0009§4.38 Inverse Hyperbolic Functions: Further Properties","header":"Power series","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___38-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions___-Further-Properties/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___38-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions___-Further-Properties--___4___38-Inverse-Hyperbolic-Functions___-Further-Properties--Power-series"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___45-Methods-of-Computation/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___45-Methods-of-Computation--___4___45-Methods-of-Computation--Real-variables___-logarithms-and-exponentials":{"contents":"Logarithms use repeated square-root reduction and scaling; exponentials use decimal rescaling so the residual argument is small.\nDLMF 4.45.E1\ny=x^{2^{-m}}-1,\\qquad \\frac{1}{10}\\leq x\\leq 10.DLMF 4.45.E2\n\\ln x=2^m\\ln(1+y).Here m is the selected number of square-root reductions in the numerical\nmethod; the formal API restricts it to a natural number, as repeated\nsquare-root steps require.\nDLMF 4.45.E3\n\\ln x=\\ln \\xi+m\\ln 10,\\qquad x=10^m\\xi,\\qquad \\frac{1}{10}\\leq \\xi\\leq 10.In this decimal-rescaling method, m is the selected integer that puts ξ\nin the stated stable range, rather than a free computational choice.\nThe reusable API names this selected index decimalReductionIndex, its\nresidual decimalReductionResidual, and the bound\ndecimalReductionRange; E4 relates those objects directly.\nDLMF 4.45.E4\nm=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{x}{\\ln 10}+\\frac12\\right\\rfloor,\\qquad y=x-m\\ln 10.DLMF 4.45.E5\ne^x=10^m e^y.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.45 Methods of Computation\u0009§4.45 Methods of Computation","header":"Real variables: logarithms and exponentials","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___45-Methods-of-Computation/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___45-Methods-of-Computation--___4___45-Methods-of-Computation--Real-variables___-logarithms-and-exponentials"},"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___18-Methods-of-Computation/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___18-Methods-of-Computation--___6___18-Methods-of-Computation--Main-functions":{"contents":"For small or moderate values of x and |z|, the power-series methods of\n§6.6 and the spherical-Bessel series of\n§6.10(ii) are available. The spherical-Bessel\nseries generally converges more rapidly; except for\n6.10.6, it also avoids cancellation when\nz=x>0.\n\nFor large x or |z|, inverse-factorial series\n(§6.10(i)) and asymptotic expansions\n(§6.12) are available, with exponential\nimprovement increasing attainable asymptotic accuracy. Other phase ranges can\nbe covered by the continuation formulas of\n§6.4.\n\nQuadrature of integral representations is another effective method. In\nparticular, the Gauss–Laguerre and Gauss–Legendre formulas of\n§3.5(v) apply to suitable representations,\nincluding 6.2.2; the source points to Todd\n(1954), Tseng and Lee (1998), and Tooper and Mark (1968) for applications.\n\nThe continued fraction 6.9.1 is useful when\n|z| is bounded away from the origin, although convergence becomes slow near\nthe negative real axis.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Exponential, Logarithmic, Sine, and Cosine Integrals\u0009§6.18 Methods of Computation\u0009§6.18 Methods of Computation","header":"Main functions","id":"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___18-Methods-of-Computation/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___18-Methods-of-Computation--___6___18-Methods-of-Computation--Main-functions"}});